(Abstract) Tj
T*
(Periapical inflammation following root canal preparation causes pain and progressive) Tj
T*
(tissue destruction. Calcium-based medications remain standard practice, though alternative) Tj
T*
(agents with immunological properties may offer complementary benefits. We compared a) Tj
T*
(statin-based gel formulation with the conventional hydroxylated-calcium standard as) Tj
T*
(intracanal medication in 30 patients with confirmed necrotic premolars and acute apical) Tj
T*
(inflammatory symptoms. Participants were randomly assigned \(n=15 per group\) to receive) Tj
T*
(either statin gel or hydroxylated-calcium following mechanical instrumentation. Pain) Tj
T*
(intensity was recorded using numerical rating scales at 6, 12, 24, and 48 hours post-) Tj
T*
(treatment. Inflammatory markers in periapical exudate were quantified at baseline and) Tj
T*
(after one week. At 6 hours, statin-treated patients reported lower pain scores compared to) Tj
T*
(hydroxylated-calcium controls. Pain ratings converged between groups by 24 hours post-) Tj
T*
(treatment. Both medications reduced inflammatory markers from baseline; statin treatment) Tj
T*
(demonstrated differential effects on different inflammatory mediators. No adverse events) Tj
T*
(were documented in either group. While both medications proved clinically effective,) Tj
T*
(preliminary findings suggest statin-based intracanal formulations warrant further) Tj
T*
(investigation as potentially useful additions to standard endodontic protocols.) Tj
